f.block('        <div class="foot">Rooftop Scout reads public records', "\n        </div>`;\n", """        <div class="foot">Rooftop Scout reads public records and models them. It does not inspect roofs, and a colour is a probability, not a finding about a household. The requests go from this browser to Esri (imagery, address search), FEMA (buildings), the Census Bureau (streets), the county assessors' public parcel layers (Los Angeles, San Diego through SANDAG, Orange, Riverside, Santa Clara, Contra Costa, San Joaquin, Sonoma, Ventura, Alameda, Sacramento, San Mateo, Placer) and San Francisco's open data, the permit files of the City and County of Los Angeles, San Francisco, Sacramento and San Jose, CAL FIRE's hosted layers (fire zones, perimeters, damage inspections), the California Energy Commission's climate zone layer, the public Overpass API servers that serve OpenStreetMap data for the 3D streets (overpass-api.de, run by FOSSGIS, then Kumi Systems' overpass.kumi.systems and VK's maps.mail.ru when it does not answer), Hugging Face and jsDelivr (once, for the AI model that traces the 3D view and its runtime), the USGS elevation service (for the photoreal view's ground), Google (for its Street View photos, while they show, and with your key its photoreal 3D tiles from tile.googleapis.com) and, with a token, Mapillary. Each request carries only the area or point in view, the parcel numbers in it, or the address you type in the search box (and, like any web request, this computer's network address). The AI detail and the AI tracing run on this computer and send nothing; what the module keeps (the last view, your field checks and notes) stays in this browser, and a key goes only to its own service. Street View is Google's own player, loaded from google.com while it shows. Building level readings are for planning field visits: under the atlas's compliance rules (module 02, What never to publish; modules 05 and 08 target ZIPs, never single homes) a specific damaged or burned home's address, photo or owner never goes into an ad, a page or a post without written permission, and after a declared disaster California's price and solicitation rules apply (module 03).</div>""")
